hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in her first  essay the Objectivist ethics in her  short work the virtue of selfishness  Iran will provide an overview of what  she calls the virtues and three of these  are what she terms cardinal virtues  we'll come to that in just a moment and  then there's several other virtues that  she explores to some degree in sort of  overviews and short summaries now why  talk in terms of virtues traditionally  virtue ethics has looked at certain  states of character as being positive  they're good for that person they lead  to happiness which Rand says is indeed  something that we ought to be seeking  and they're also understood as being  good per se and being good for the  matrix of relationships and society that  we live in and they also represent a  full development of what a human being  is capable of so they realize our nature  they also structure our perceptions and  our Volition's they are habits that are  built over time or just  positions so this is a robust conception  of virtue and I think that Rand really  does have something like that in mind  although she's probably less worried  about whether they are good for other  people and much more worried about  whether they're good for the individual  who has them or the people that he or  she chooses to view is important in  their life spouses children friends  neighbors etc so what are virtues doing  in her moral theory she tells us that  value is that which one acts to gain and  or to keep so value is is something  fundamental in her ethics virtues she  says are the act by which one gains and  or keeps it now of course act is a  little bit too narrow so we have to like  read her a little bit against herself  here these are not simply actions these  are characteristics of people modes of  action and tension allottee all those  sorts of things that you know we would  normally think of as involved in in  virtuous virtues do culminate in acts  but they're not simply acts so the  virtues are that which allow us to  realize our values and then she says the  three cardinal values of the Objectivist  ethics the three values which together  our means to in the realization of one's  ultimate value one's own life and a  little bit later she'll say one's own  happiness our reason purpose and self  esteem now that you sound like you know  good things and indeed they they  generally are she says that each of  these leads us to a particular virtue so  reason as a value is something that we  want to have something we want to keep  something we want to acquire is embodied  in rationality right very similar  purpose  is embodied in productiveness or as  she's going to say a little bit later  productive work self-esteem is going to  be manifested in the disposition or  virtue of pride so these are the three  key virtues and she says that productive  work or productiveness is the central  purpose of a rational person's life the  central value that integrates and  determines the hierarchy of all their  other values then she says reason is the  source the precondition of their  productive work and pride is the result  so we can say that these these three  virtues and their values are connected  together in certain ways  rationality she's going to say is the  source of the precondition of productive  work you have to be rational you have to  use your rationality in order to do that  it's also she's going to say the the  source of the other virtues that we're  going to discuss which she places under  under rationality and her discussion  self-esteem leads to pride pride as  she's going to call it around a moral  ambitiousness is the result of these  these other things so you could see it  as sort of the capstone and then  productiveness is essentially the means  by which we we realize this right it's  the central purpose of a rational  person's life it integrates and  determines the hierarchy of the other  values as she just said so it's it's  performing a valuable function so all  three of these are connected together in  rands  view let's take a look at what she has  to say about each of these and actually  let's let's begin with self esteem let's  begin at the end because she's gonna say  an awful lot about rationality rather  self esteem which leads to - there's  embodied in Pride so she says the virtue  of pride is the recognition of the fact  that as man must produce the physical  values he needs to sustain his life so  must he acquire the values of character  that make his life worth sustaining  that's talking about a certain condition  for moral existence right so what would  pry to actually be it would be having or  at least being on the way to trying to  acquire those valuable characteristics  those features of one's character that  make a person valuable to themself where  they can look you know for verbally look  at themselves in the mirror and like  what they see not because their face is  attractive but because they like the  person that they they are so she says  that a person is a being of self-made  wealth and they're a being of self-made  soul the virtue of pride can be best  described by the term moral  ambitiousness and then she gives you a  you know sort of a rundown of that it  means that one must earn the right to  hold oneself as one's highest value by  achieving one's own moral perfection and  you do this by never accepting any code  of irrational virtues impossible to  practice never failing to practice the  virtues you know to be rational never  accepting an unearned guilt and never  earning any it was we can go on and on  and and so these are you know rands  point of view you know it's rooted in an  Objectivist egoism but the idea here is  that self-esteem isn't just something  you start out with it's something that  you earn and so pride would be this  ambition of being a better person being  the kind of person who's not to use one  of her favorite words a moocher alright  somebody who sponges off of others so  you know that could be furthered in a  lot of ways I remember when I was a kid  and I would go over to my friends house  for the weekend we engage in a lot of  fantasy war gaming using miniature  figures and his parents were much much  you know better off than my mother was  and my mom would would send me over with  a casserole or with some sort of dessert  and I would give it to my friend  George's mom and she would like look at  it and she feels like you know you don't  have to bring all this stuff and I would  say well my mom wanted you know me to  give this to you so that we wouldn't  just be and I forget exactly what the  term was but it was something like a  moocher somebody who who is a parasite  who lives off the largesse of others and  it was kind of a running joke at the  time but that embodies something that  that Rand is actually talking about here  with pride not only is it about having  wealth that you've developed yourself  it's about having wealth of character  that you've developed yourself and  there's many people who have wealth  they've got a lot of zeroes behind the  numbers in their bank account but there  are complete pieces of garbage as human  